WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
A practical playbook review starts with scenario and scope. What incident class does this

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.180
playbook cover?

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.360
What is out of scope? What triggers start the workflow?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:15.020
The trigger might be an alert, report, external notification, suspicious observation,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.240
or confirmed incident pattern.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.860
The playbook should be clear enough that a responder knows when to use it and when to move

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.440
to a different playbook.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.860
Next, check ownership. Who triages the case?

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.000
Who owns the affected systems, identities, cloud resources,

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.260
or business service? Who can approve containment?

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.400
Who handles legal, privacy, HR, communications,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.340
or executive escalation when needed? A playbook without owners will slow down during the

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.880
exact moment it is supposed to help.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:51.300
Then review data sources and evidence. The playbook should list the logs, telemetry,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.800
tools, dashboards, repositories, and case records needed for the scenario.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.820
It should tell responders what to document: timestamps, systems, accounts,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.800
evidence sources, actions, approvals, decisions, and open questions.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.900
It should use careful language such as observed, suspected, confirmed, contained,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.440
recovered, and unresolved.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:20.060
If chain of custody may matter, the playbook should explain how evidence is stored,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.440
transferred, and protected.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:27.460
The response flow should support triage and severity classification. It should help

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.680
responders ask what happened,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:33.260
who and what are affected, whether activity is active,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.360
whether sensitive data or critical services are involved,

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.260
and whether containment is urgent.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.600
Severity should guide urgency and escalation without replacing analysis. Containment,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.700
eradication, and recovery should be practical.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.440
The playbook should list safe containment options, their tradeoffs,

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:52.660
and approval requirements.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.840
It should explain how to remove the foothold, malicious artifact, unauthorized access,

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.700
persistence, or exploited weakness.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:05.240
Recovery should include verification, monitoring, stakeholder updates,

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.220
and clear closure criteria. Finally, check improvement.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.080
Does the playbook include post-incident review tasks, lessons learned, control updates,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.300
detection tuning, documentation cleanup, and ownership for follow-up work?

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:22.360
Has the playbook been tested in a tabletop exercise or real operation?

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.940
Is it updated when tools, teams, systems,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.800
or obligations change? Good incident response playbooks are not static paperwork.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:37.340
They are owned, tested, updated, and used. They reduce confusion, preserve evidence,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:40.440
coordinate stakeholders, and support trained judgment.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.460
The engineering checklist is compact: define the scenario, identify triggers,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.360
assign roles, list data sources, triage and classify severity, contain safely,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.460
eradicate root cause, recover systems, communicate clearly, document evidence,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.640
close the case, and improve the response model.
